        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Dylan Van Assche work for?\n\nAnswer A: Ghent University\n  about: Dylan Van Assche\n  sense: workplace\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Van Assche has worked as a researcher at Ghent University, a Dutch-speaking university in Belgium.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Humboldt University of Berlin\n  about: Dylan Van Assche\n  sense: workplace\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"During his time at the university, he was involved in several projects aimed at developing innovative applications of Semantic Web technologies.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a potential Wikipedia entry that presents comprehensive evidence for the statement:\n\nDylan Van Assche\n\nDylan Van Assche is a PhD student and Semantic Web researcher who has worked at Ghent University. Early Life and Education\n\nDylan Van Assche is a Belgian researcher who pursued his higher education in computer science. He is currently a PhD student, focusing on Semantic Web research. Career\n\nVan Assche has worked as a researcher at Ghent University, a Dutch-speaking university in Belgium. During his tenure at the university, he contributed to various projects related to Semantic Web and artificial intelligence. Evidence of Employment\n\nAccording to his LinkedIn profile, Van Assche has listed Ghent University as one of his work experiences. [1] Additionally, his research papers and publications have been affiliated with Ghent University, further substantiating his employment at the institution. [2][3]\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Van Assche, D. (n.d.). LinkedIn Profile. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.linkedin.com\\/in\\/dylanvanassche\\/>\n\n[2] Van Assche, D., & Verborgh, R. (2020). A Study on the Adoption of Semantic Web Technologies in Industry. Journal of Web Semantics, 65, 100-115. doi: 10.1016\\/j.websem.2020.02.002\n\n[3] Van Assche, D., De Meester, B., & De Vocht, L. (2019). Ontology-Based Data Integration for Industrial Applications. Proceedings of the 18th International Semantic Web Conference, 231-246. doi: 10.1007\\/978-3-030-30796-7_14\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\nDocument 2: Dylan Van Assche\n\nDylan Van Assche is a renowned PhD student and Semantic Web researcher, notable for his contributions to the field of artificial intelligence and his affiliation with the Humboldt University of Berlin. Early Life and Education\n\nVan Assche was born in 1992 in Berlin, Germany. He developed an interest in computer science at a young age and pursued his undergraduate degree in the field from the Technical University of Berlin. After completing his undergraduate studies, Van Assche was accepted into the PhD program at the Humboldt University of Berlin, where he began his research on Semantic Web technologies. Career\n\nVan Assche's research focus on Semantic Web technologies led him to work closely with the Humboldt University of Berlin's Department of Computer Science. During his time at the university, he was involved in several projects aimed at developing innovative applications of Semantic Web technologies. In 2018, Van Assche was part of a research team that published a study on \"Ontology-Based Information Retrieval Systems\" in the Journal of Web Semantics. [1] The study explored the potential of ontology-based systems in improving information retrieval on the web. Van Assche's contributions to the study were instrumental in its success, and it has since been cited over 50 times. In 2020, Van Assche presented a paper on \"Semantic Web Technologies for Artificial Intelligence\" at the International Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ICAI). [2] The paper discussed the role of Semantic Web technologies in enhancing artificial intelligence systems and was well-received by the academic community. Affiliation with Humboldt University of Berlin\n\nVan Assche's affiliation with the Humboldt University of Berlin is well-documented. According to the university's website, Van Assche was a PhD student in the Department of Computer Science from 2016 to 2020. [3] During his time at the university, he worked under the supervision of Dr. Maria Rodriguez, a renowned expert in the field of artificial intelligence. References\n\n[1] Rodriguez, M., Van Assche, D., & Schmidt, A. (2018). Ontology-Based Information Retrieval Systems. Journal of Web Semantics, 46, 12-25. [2] Van Assche, D. (2020). Semantic Web Technologies for Artificial Intelligence. Proceedings of the International Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ICAI), 123-135. [3] Humboldt University of Berlin. (2020). Department of Computer Science: PhD Students.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
